Date: Thu, 7 Jan 93 9:40:49 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Re: How Much of the Extropian Filter Have We Passed?

Mike Wiik writes:

> Perhaps, but since the universe isn't filled with gray goo then would
> a "realistic" assumption mean that there is no intelligent life beyond
> Earth? I find the latter scenario unrealistic. Perhaps there is some
> yet-to-be-discovered mechanism which prevents Von Neumann machines from
> filling up the cosmos. As unscientific as that appears, it still seems
> more probable to me than the idea that we're the 1st intelligent species...

That the lack of von Neumann machines, aliens, artifacts, goo, etc.
implies we are probably _alone_, at least in our region encompassing
the Local Group of galaxies, is precisely the point being made here.
That is, if intelligence is fairly ubiquitous (e.g., hundreds of
civilizations per standard galaxy) then one must expect, on average,
many of them to have evolved _before_ us. Fermi's question was simple:
why aren't they here?

The Barrow and Tipler book, "The Anthropic Cosmological Principle,"
considers many aspects of this problem. I last read it in 1986, but
have recently seen a paperback version (finally) released. Strongly
recommended! (And reviewed by Dave Krieger in "Extropy" #9.)

If there are on the order of 10-30 civilizations per galaxy (in some
sense of "potentially existing" from the distant past to the distant
future), then we would expect at least several of them to have already
existed for hundreds of millions of years, by simple statistics. Where
are they?

And the "Star Trek" scenario, of hundreds of alien civilizations all
in proximity to one another, is absurd beyond reckoning.

But these are the arguments made so many times on this list, so I'll
stop here.

Personally, I think the available evidence supports the scenario that
we are the first civilization to emerge in our part of the Universe.
And very possibly the _only_ civilization in this galaxy, in this
Local Group, and perhaps in the entire universe.


-Tim


