Date: Thu, 7 Jan 93 10:54:21 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Re: How Much of the Extropian Filter Have We Passed?

> How can you tell the difference between dark matter in the Universe and
> grey goo?
> 
> Dani Eder

Amara G. may have more current views, but I'll take a shot anyway:

1. Most of the "missing matter" (the estimated 80-90% of the total
mass of the universe that is not visible to us currently) is _not_
thought to be in the active portions of galaxies. This is as estimated
from the apparent rotational rates of galaxies, the so-called virial
theorem, etc. The missing mass = dark matter (for this discussion) is
thus expected to be in one of several places:

a. in galactic haloes or peripheral regions of galaxies.

b. as large clouds of dark matter (gas, brown dwarfs, whatever...)
between the galaxies (this possibility was recently given much more
support by the apparent existence of large clouds exerting an
influence on clusters of galaxies).

c. as some kind of massive particle or other exotic particle which
permeates the universe but which is hard to detect (cf. "WIMPS," for
"weakly-interacting massive particles"...contrasted with "MACHOS,"
which I won't go into further here..."Discover" and "Scientific
American" have had several good articles on this recently).

2. One would expect civilizations capable of developing grey goo to
have developed in the active regions of galaxies, as opposed to out
where the stars _aren't_. But this is, by #1 above, not where the
missing mass is.

3. Grey goo, in the form of von Neumann machines restructuring stars
(the implicit assumption in Dani's speculation that the missing = dark
matter is grey goo), would be expected to start somewhere and
spread. That is, it is likely to have visible nonhomogeneities, such as
clearly apparent voids with geometric shapes (e.g., spherical).

The apparent uniformity of dark matter around us (not fully mapped,
I'll grant, so this argument is fairly weak) suggests that if the dark
matter is somehow grey goo, then our portion of the universe got
spared. (A lot of variations on this argument, dealing with the
restructuring times Hal Finney mentioned, but the basic point seems
valid.)

4. Most speculatively, I would expect grey goo, even out-of-control
grey goo, to have been initiated to "do" something. This is
inconsistent with dark matter all around us just sitting there! (Even
if _most_ of the dark matter is "metastasized goo," like a cancer, one
would expect _some_ of it to be structured and "purposive.")

A long answer to a short question...now you see another reason I took
a vacation from posting.

-Tim


