Date: Thu, 7 Jan 93 19:40:17 -0800
From: David Jevans <jevans@apple.com>
Subject: Shamen, History, Wanking

This message deals with several issues related to extra-terrestrials,
extropian filters, history, nanotech, social development, etc.

Caveat:
Please don't get me wrong.  I'm a member of ExI and I agree with most
of what comes across this list, but I have a few meta-points that Tim
May's recent compressed message stimulated me into posting.

-----

1.  The Shamen lyrics that Betsy posted are by Terrence McKenna, who is NOT
a member of the band.  McKenna is a self-professed "shamanologist".  He
has been active in the fields of research into psychedelics, philosophy,
religion, futurism, singularity, and self-transformation for many years.
His brother Dennis is a big psychedelics researcher (;-)  I have tapes
of Terrence McKenna speaking on KFPA.  Brilliant man.  Don't confuse him
with some new-age crackpot.

2.  In many extropian discussions on this list people claim "history
shows that..." xyz.  Anything from weapons development to evolution
being space filling.  What a crock!  As far as weapons of mass destruction,
we've only had them 100 years!  Nukes for 50 years!  Human history is
only a couple of thousand years old.  Appeal to historical precedent is
pretty vapid when discussing extra-terrestrials and future civilizations.
And evolution being space-filling?  Where are the 3 legged, 10 headed,
twelve penised creatures?  Evolution is the result of initial conditions
and the environment.  The Earth (and any other environment) is pretty
particular, as are initial conditions.  Evolution is actually pretty narrow
in its scope.

3.  When discussing extra-terrestrials, we are all talking about them in
human terms.  Have you ever stopped to think that perhaps some alien
might be so f*cking alien that they wouldn't have stupid ideas like
free markets and mathematics and McDonalds?

4.  On a related note, what if our physical manifestation defines what
the Universe is (to us)?  There could easily be concious beings existing
on some so-called "other plane of existence", a "parallel universe", that
we and our instruments (which reflect our human construction just as
language does, just as clothes do) cannot register their existence?

5.  Perhaps aliens, and we too, actually evolve a concience.  ie. we start
to give a shit about others around us, the third world, etc.  We get so
busy taking care of others that we delay technology for a few million years
or so?

6.  Maybe we realize that we aren't that smart.  Ever thought about your
own intelligence?  How many things do you know?  It seems like maybe 10
or 20 or something, because my conciousness can't see a list or any kind
of representation of the things that I know.  Now when I actually start doing
something or talking or writing then information flows like crazy.  Where is
it stored? Why do we forget?  Why do we make mistakes?  Why do we disagree?

Why do we let corporations, mass media, and career politicians run the world?
Who the f*ck put a nuclear armada in the hands of an 80-year old ex-actor?

The point is that it appears to me that conciousness is so tiny, that we
have evolved only the barest little bit of awareness.  Unfortunately we
can build things that we don't know how to control yet.  Like nukes.
Like big corporations and nations of hundreds of millions of people.
Like email.

Perhaps conciousness and evolution and expansion into the universe follow
formulae, just like pi and e=mc^2, and given our level of conciousness and
our fascination with mechanical stuff, that we are bound to not get out of
this galaxy (at least not for some time).

Perhaps conciousness will never improve.  I can image computer systems so
different from today, with hand-waving to "program" them, self-aware computers,
completely graphical VR computers, with the entire earth and every individual
networked together.  Yet we are stumped by the halting problem, and 90 million
people still use DOS!

7.  Maybe nanotech is indeed a singularity, but not a good one.  When the first
nuclear bomb was exploded, no one was sure that it wouldn't cause a reation
in the rest of the world and blow the whole place up.  Now, with nanotech,
we can be reasonably sure that the first Von Neumann machine is indeed
going to replicate like crazy and fill the world up!  Yikes!

8.  Perhaps a free market system results in catastrophe.  Oil tanker spills.
Nuclear accident.  Pollution.  AIDS or other fatal virus.  There are so
many disaster scenarios that a free market system makes possible, almost
certain.  "Let the market decide".  That pretty much admits that humans
are incapable of planning, doesn't it!  Once you have global effects from
individuals screwing up, you can't just let the market decide.  Some
Bozo is gonna natural select himself, and take us all with him.

9.  Have any of you had sex recently?  Gone for a hike in the woods?
Visited an art gallery or attended a concert?  Ridden a motorcycle up
the Oregon coast?  Read some Eastern philosophy?  These things are all
extremely pleasant, and are a part of our everyday reality.  Since we've got
to live in this world for some time, perhaps we ought to get in touch with it.
There is a lot to be learned out there.  Pure extropianism is as
narrow minded a view as any other.  Since you haven't experienced other
lifestyles, how can you be so sure that extropianism is right?  As any
creative thinker knows, multiple varied inputs lead to creativity

10.  There is a group called "Global Awareness Through Experience".  Their
thesis is that symbols (such as writing, picutres, newscasts) don't
actually convey the meaning of powerful concepts such as starvation,
disease, hatred, war, etc.  This group takes people to visit places like
Mexico City to actually see what these words MEAN.  Perhaps we ought to go
do this and then reevaluate our philosophies?

11.  Why aren't you doing something?  Very few of you are actually DOING
anything other than reading and posting (my congrats however to the ExI crew
and the various hardcopy authors out there).  It is far more important in
life to actually DO something than to simply wank on and on thinking about it.

12.  To reduce noise on this list, simply stop posting it.

Summary

What I'm proposing is that we stop our mad dash into technocracy and think
about ourselves objectively.  Can we handle this technological obsession?
Don't we have to deal with the social aspects and problems (talk about
difficult problems!  Moving a galaxy around is easier than solving
world hunger!) before rushing headlong into VR anal-retentive-space?

If you build technology, and put it in the hands of politicians, corporations,
and 19 year olds, what do you think they'll do?  Use it against you to
maintain the status quo.

Think.  Explore.  Discover the rich beauty of this world before moving onto/
into the next.  While Extropianism may be the answer, what was the question?

-dj

