Date: Thu, 7 Jan 93 20:31:24 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Re: Shamen, History, Wanking

David Jevans has posted a thought-provoking essay. I salute him for
raising some important issues about our goals, our attraction to
technology, and possible wrong paths.

IMHO, _this_ kind of "challenging the basics" is very appropriate for
this group. No negativism or anger, just some questions.

My own caveat: I disagree, though, with David's comment about our
seeming inability to help the poor and the Third World, and I can't
imagine that "helping the poor" could possibly sidetrack the
development of technology for even a few decades, let alone the
semi-permanent sidetracking David mentioned. In fact, technological
development, while not perfect, and not desirable to all, has generally
been the path out of poverty for most of the world. Also,
technological development leads to easier working lives and to the
"demographic transition" which causes population pressures to ease.

However, the dangers of being sucked into a world of e-mail, posting to the
Net, and obsessing over the latest software releases are very real. I
have also commented on this, including my recent reference to
Extropianism possibly being harmful, in the sense of sapping creative
impulses and making short-term work seem boring and meaningless.

While I will refrain from commenting on my personal life (such as the
last time I had sex or the last time I walked in the woods...at least
one of these things I did quite recently :-} ), I will say I avoid the
techno-punk, hyper-glitz lifestyle as much as possible. I moved out to
the Santa Cruz area specifically to be outside the Silicon Valley
rat-race--and yet close enough to visit occasionally. I cherish my
open beam ceilings, my redwoods in the back yard, and my (small) view
of the ocean.

"Life in the fast lane" must be balanced by more natural, authentic
experiences. (On a related note, the inventor of the idea of an
"authentic life," Martin Heidegger--himself the subject of a discussion on
this list several weeks ago--lived out the last decades of his life in
relative isolation in the Black Forest.)

Friends of mine have vacated the inner cities and barren suburbs for
the peace of the hills of Marin, Sonoma, Napa, and Santa Cruz.
Complete with high-speed telecom links, fax machines, satellite
dishes, and powerful work-at-home tools (some friends of mine even do
complete chip designs at home, fab out prototypes at MOSIS, debug the
chips in their own lab, and still manage to live out of sight of any
other houses...the long-predicted "electronic cottage" is arriving).

I wonder if some of the visions of an Extropian future have to do with
the "rave" culture (house music, all-night rhythmic movement,
techno-pop, resurgence of LSD) that seems to be growing exponentially?
I offer this as mere speculation, and would welcome comments from some
of the "slackers" amongst you (Generation X, twentysomethings, Baby
Busters...).

Again, thanks, David, for raising a genuinely new topic on this list.

-Tim May

