One comment re galaxies.  We may conclude that we are the only intelligent
species in the galaxy.  But intergalactic travel should be easier, compared
to interstellar travel, than interstellar travel is compared to inter-
planetary travel.  There are convenient local dwarf galaxies as well as
large galaxies like Andromeda in the local group.  And there are other
groups nearby, providing many stepping stones to the universe.

Within about 100 million light years, visitable in less than 1% of the
age of the universe, there are a lot of galaxies, judging from a
picture I was looking at in the book, Galaxies.  Maybe a million of
them?  Maybe even more...

The same kind of reasoning that rids our galaxy of intelligent life
applies, although with somewhat less force, to the closest million
galaxies or so.  Add another factor of a million to Robin's extropian
filter.

