Tim responded so nicely to David Jevans, <jevans@apple.com>, but I
found much about David's post that I disagreed with.

> 6.  Maybe we realize that we aren't that smart.  Ever thought about your
> own intelligence?

Improvement of our minds is one of the main Extropian goals.  We have
had many discussions on this list about the nature of consciousness
and intelligence, and the implications for various forms of
technological improvement that may become possible.

> The point is that it appears to me that conciousness is so tiny, that we
> have evolved only the barest little bit of awareness.  Unfortunately we
> can build things that we don't know how to control yet.  Like nukes.
> Like big corporations and nations of hundreds of millions of people.

I'm not comfortable with this notion that "we" have to control the
things you mention.  Notice that some of these things are other
people.  Do you want to control other people?

Wouldn't it be better for people to be free to make their own
decisions, even when the results are messy and don't fit your
preconceived notions of a nicely controlled system?

> 8.  Perhaps a free market system results in catastrophe.  Oil tanker spills.  
> Nuclear accident.  Pollution.  AIDS or other fatal virus.  There are so 
> many disaster scenarios that a free market system makes possible, almost 
> certain.

It's a common misconception that government planning protects the
environment while free market systems destroy it.  But actually, the
least free countries have had the worst environmental records in the
world.  Eastern Europe and the Soviet Union are decades behind the
West.

The Aral Sea is an environmental disaster caused by misguided
government planning.  The rivers that feed it have been diverted,
causing it to shrink dramatically, ruining the economies of former
port (now desert) towns.  And like all the seas in the area, the lake
was badly polluted.  The newly exposed lake bed dries and is blown by
the wind, spreading toxic dust.

And of course the Chernobyl nuclear accident occured under this same
system of strong government planning.  It seems to me that it's
government control, not free markets, that makes disasters likely.

> "Let the market decide".  That pretty much admits that humans 
> are incapable of planning, doesn't it!  Once you have global effects from 
> individuals screwing up, you can't just let the market decide.  Some 
> Bozo is gonna natural select himself, and take us all with him.  

This "planning" you call for is what has led to so much trouble in the
world.  "Let the market decide" really means, "let individuals
decide".  Why do you suppose that government planning has done so badly
over the years?

> Pure extropianism is as 
> narrow minded a view as any other.  Since you haven't experienced other 
> lifestyles, how can you be so sure that extropianism is right?  As any 
> creative thinker knows, multiple varied inputs lead to creativity 

How do you know that people here haven't experienced other lifestyles?
You don't know us.

I really resent it when people come on the list and start making
blanket statements about list members that are insulting and
inaccurate.  Mike Wiik did the same thing.

My theory is that people have a certain preconception of what kind of
person is interested in the things we talk about here.  But I think
that preconception is terribly inaccurate.  Maybe this is related to
the point I made a couple of weeks ago, that we are not "defined" by
an interest in gee-whiz high-tech gadgets.  Rather, our interest is
and should be, IMO, in a shared philosophical perspective on the
opportunities that will be coming during our lifetimes.

We are not a bunch of wild-eyed, callow technophiles.  We are not
mostly college students.  We are not fanatics.

What we are, in my opinion, are individuals who share a certain view
of what technology is going to make possible in the next century and
beyond, along with a determination to use these new tools to improve
ourselves and expand our possibilities.  Why people conclude from this
that we never have sex or go for walks in the woods is beyond me.

The fact is, many or most of the people on this list are into physical
fitness and exercise.  Improving our bodies as well as our minds is
part of the extropian lifestyle, as is enjoying all that life has to
offer.  The first event I was invited to by other extropians was a
hike in the mountains.

Maybe the people who are attracted to the gee-whiz tech stuff do fit
the stereotype that David, Mike Wiik, and some others seem to have
had.  I don't mean to be making any accusations here, but I'm just
wondering where this image comes from.  I recall when Steve Witham
appeared and called himself "FutureNerd", I was very surprised that
someone perceived the list in that light.  Maybe there are some people
here who do nothing but sit at their computers, but this is not, IMO,
consistent with Extropian philosophy and goals.

Perhaps some of the non-extropian technophiles on the list would care
to comment on whether David's descriptions apply more closely to
themselves.

> Summary 
> 
> What I'm proposing is that we stop our mad dash into technocracy and think 
> about ourselves objectively.  Can we handle this technological obsession?  

Technology need not be seen as the threat that you seem to imply.  What
if your ancestors had stopped their mad dash into technology hundreds
of years ago?  Today you would be suffering with smallpox and
malnutrition.  I'm sure that some years from now we will look back at
the state of today's technology as similarly primitive.  We will pity
those who had to live under the hardships which we know as our daily
life.

> Think.  Explore.  Discover the rich beauty of this world before moving onto/ 
> into the next.  While Extropianism may be the answer, what was the question?  

The question was, where will we be in 30, 50, 100 years?  Unless we
think about that now, we won't be able to take best advantage of the
opportunities that lie ahead.  And the kind of exploration you mention
is perfectly in keeping with Extropian ideas of self transformation
and expansion.

If you stay with the list, I think you will find that there is much
more here than idle speculations and political arguments.  There are
some very smart people posting on this list, and it can be a valuable
resource to help you prepare for the future.

